===Motivation===

Since Bitcoin v0.3.1 (addressing CVE-2010-5137), Bitcoin's scripting capabilities have been significantly restricted to mitigate known vulnerabilities related to excessive computational time and memory usage.  These early safeguards were necessary to prevent denial-of-service attacks and ensure the stability and reliability of the Bitcoin network.

However, as Bitcoin usage becomes more sophisticated, these limitations are becoming more salient.  New proposals often must explicitly address potential performance pitfalls by severely limiting their scope, introducing specialized caching strategies to mitigate execution costs, or using the existing sigops budget in ad-hoc ways to enforce dynamic execution limits.

This BIP introduces a simple, systematic and explicit cost framework for evaluating script operations based on stack data interactions, using worst-case behavior as the limiting factor.  Even with these pessimistic assumptions, large classes of scripts can be shown to be within budget (for all possible inputs) by static analysis.

===A Model For Opcodes Dealing With Stack Data===

Without an explicit and low limit on the size of stack operands, the bottleneck for script operations is based on the time taken to process the stack data it accesses (with the exception of signature operations).  The cost model uses the length of the stack inputs (or occasionally, outputs), hence the term "varops".

* Block weight limits directly encoded script size, but future script compression may increase logical opcode density without proportionally increasing transaction weight.
* Every logical opcode execution therefore has a minimum cost, preventing script compression from bypassing the varops budget.
* Work that scales with stack data is charged separately, including the storage lifetime of every value written.
* We assume implementations use simple linear arrays/vectors of contiguous memory.
* We assume implementations use linear accesses to stack data (perhaps multiple times): random accesses would require an extension to the model.
* We assume object size is limited to the entire transaction (4,000,000 bytes, worst-case).
* Costs are based on the worst-case behavior of each opcode.

The last two assumptions make a large difference in practice: normal usage on small, cache-hot objects is much faster than this model suggests.  But an implementation which is more efficient than the versions modeled does not introduce any problems: only an implementation with a significantly worse worst-case behavior would be problematic.

==Design==

A per-transaction integer "varops budget" is determined by multiplying the total transaction weight by the fixed factor 10,000 (chosen to make operation costs all integer values).  It is meant for Tapleaf 0xC2 inputs, defined by [[bip-0441.mediawiki|BIP441]], and for leaf and witness versions not yet defined, but must be kept separate from the existing input types, which use the sigops budget instead and are not costed by the varops budget:
* legacy and witness version 0 inputs (block's sigops limit)
* Tapleaf 0xC0 inputs, ([[bip-0342.mediawiki|BIP342]] sigops budget)
* Taproot key-path spends, to one signature each

The weight of these inputs is not counted: if it also funded the varops budget, a block could add the signature checks the varops budget allows to theirs, exceeding today's worst case.  All other inputs fund the budget.

For this calculation, an input's weight is the serialized size of its outpoint, scriptSig, and sequence multiplied by the witness discount factor, plus the serialized size of its witness stack; all CompactSize prefixes are included.

The budget is <code>10000 * (weight(tx) - excluded_weight)</code>, where <code>weight(tx)</code> is the transaction weight defined by [[bip-0141.mediawiki|BIP141]] and <code>excluded_weight</code> is the sum of the weights of the excluded inputs.

Each funding input that spends a Taproot script path pays one <code>SIGCHECK</code> from the budget for its [[bip-0341.mediawiki|BIP341]] commitment check.  Its whole weight, including the non-witness part, already funds signature checks at the full rate, so without this charge the commitment check would come on top, and a block of small script-path inputs could exceed today's worst case.

A later version may exclude its inputs from the budget or charge against it, since either only restricts validity, but weight that funds this budget must not also fund a signature check that is not charged to it.

The budget is transaction-wide (rather than per-input) to allow for cross-input introspection: a small input may reasonably access larger inputs.  The resulting cross-input dependency is described under [[#security-considerations|Security Considerations]].

A transaction is invalid if the sum of its inputs' charges exceeds the budget, whatever order the inputs are evaluated in.  Inputs evaluated concurrently may each be checked against the budget that remained when their evaluation started, with their charges deducted once it ends; the deduction that would exceed the budget fails.

Each logical opcode execution is charged.  Because a linear operation determines its exact cost as it executes, that cost may be deducted afterward instead of being calculated in a separate pass; exceeding the remaining budget invalidates the script before the next opcode.  Superlinear operations and signature checks must instead be charged before execution because they could otherwise perform disproportionately expensive work before an overrun is detected.  Operations whose result can be longer than the stack elements they read, with a length set by an operand's value, must likewise be charged before execution.

===Derivation of Costs===

The costs of opcodes were determined by benchmarking on a variety of platforms.  

The goal is that the varops budget introduces no new worst-case validation time.  Signature checks in legacy and witness version 0 inputs are limited per block, not by weight, and their weight does not fund the varops budget, so the slowest blocks share that part.  The varops budget is therefore calibrated so that no Tapleaf 0xC2 workload is slower than the slowest Tapleaf 0xC0 workload of the same weight.  The slowest Tapleaf 0xC0 workloads are dominated by:
* Schnorr signature validation (80,000 signatures per block)
* Repeated hashing of 520-byte elements (3DUP + HASH256, 3DUP + RIPEMD160)

To estimate a conservative maximum runtime for each opcode, we assume a script consumes the varops budget of a whole block, 10,000 * 4,000,000 (40b), whatever its size: each workload is repeated and its runtime normalized to that budget, without the weight limit on script size.  The script is assumed to execute in a single thread, and preparing its initial stack is not timed.

Each cost primitive is benchmarked on each machine across its operand sizes, fitted, combined into the cheapest formula that covers every machine, and rounded up; a primitive that covers several operations, such as HASH, covers each of them.  Complete scripts for every opcode, and searches for their slowest operand shapes, then check the costs against the slowest Tapleaf 0xC0 workload on each machine.

The signature cost is simply carried across from the existing [[bip-0342.mediawiki|BIP-342]] limit: 50 weight units allow you one signature.  Since each transaction gets varops budget for the funding weight of the entire transaction (not just the current input's witness), and each input has at least 41 bytes (164 weight), this is actually slightly more generous than the sigops budget (which was 50 + witness weight), but still limits the signatures it funds to 80,000 per block.  SIGCHECK is therefore a price carried over from BIP342, not a fitted one.  Every other elliptic-curve operation is also charged one SIGCHECK: a public key tweak, as in a Taproot commitment check, takes about 0.8 of the time of a Schnorr verification on the benchmark machines, so one price covers both with a small overcharge.

The raw benchmark data, analysis scripts, and visualized results are available at https://github.com/jmoik/varopsData, whose [https://github.com/jmoik/varopsData/blob/master/METHODOLOGY.md METHODOLOGY.md] describes each step in detail.  This repository also provides instructions for running the benchmarks on your own machine.

===Cost Primitives===

An opcode's charge is the sum of the applicable primitives below.

The primitives take the following arguments:

* <code>n</code>: byte length of the data the primitive processes
* <code>W(n) = 8 * ceil(n / 8)</code>, the wordspan: bytes in the 64-bit words that hold <code>n</code> bytes
* <code>H(n) = 64 * ceil((n + 9) / 64)</code>: bytes a hash with 64-byte blocks processes, the message and at least 9 bytes of padding and length
* <code>k</code>: number of entries an opcode takes off the top of a stack and puts back
* <code>n, m</code>: byte lengths of the longer and shorter operand of a multiplication, or of the dividend and divisor of a division
* <code>Q(n, m) = MAX(0, W(n) - W(m))</code>: bytes by which a dividend's word span exceeds its divisor's; a division makes one row per 64-bit word of it

The opcode formulas specify each argument from the opcode's operands.  Each formula has a constant term and grows with its arguments as its algorithm's running time does: linearly for READ, WRITE, ARITH, MOVE and HASH, and quadratically, through a product of two word-span terms, for MUL and DIV; BASE and SIGCHECK are constant.

{|
! Primitive and cost (varops)
! Included work
|-
|<code>BASE = 300</code>
|Common instruction decoding, dispatch, cost deduction and stack-limit checks.
|-
|<code>READ(n) = 350 + 2 W(n)</code>
|Take an <code>n</code>-byte operand from the stack as an unsigned number in 64-bit words, or read it as bytes, and inspect it: compare it, test it for zero, find its trailing zero bytes or take it as a length or index.
|-
|<code>WRITE(n) = 800 + 7 W(n)</code>
|Produce one stack value of <code>n</code> bytes and place it on the stack, including its eventual removal: copy or build its bytes, including converting a numeric result to its bytes where needed.
|-
|<code>ARITH(n) = 200 + 3 W(n)</code>
|One linear pass over the 64-bit words of an <code>n</code>-byte span, each result word computed from a fixed number of input words, with or without a carry or borrow from the previous word: addition, subtraction, bitwise logic, bit shifts, byte moves and byte reversal.  Includes loop setup and dropping the result's trailing zero bytes.
|-
|<code>MOVE(k) = 200 + 23 k</code>
|Take the top <code>k</code> entries off a stack and put back some or all of them, in any order and on either stack, without reading or copying their contents.
|-
|<code>MUL(n,m) = 700 + W(n) + 18 W(m) + W(n) W(m)</code>
|Schoolbook multiplication of an <code>n</code>-byte operand by an <code>m</code>-byte operand, <code>n &ge; m</code>: one row per 64-bit word of the shorter operand, each multiplying the words of the longer operand by that word and adding the row and its carry into the product.  Includes setup, a pass over the longer operand and internal scratch storage.
|-
|<code>DIV(n,m) = 1150 + 60 Q(n, m) + 11 W(m) + Q(n, m) W(m)</code>
|Division or remainder of an <code>n</code>-byte dividend by an <code>m</code>-byte divisor: estimate quotient words and multiply/subtract and correct the remainder, modeled as one row over the divisor's words per word of <code>Q(n, m)</code>, plus a fixed few, which the constant and the <code>W(m)</code> term pay for.  Internal normalization, temporary storage and dropping the result's trailing zero bytes are included.
|-
|<code>HASH(n) = 50 + 40 H(n)</code>
|A complete SHA256, RIPEMD160 or SHA1 hash of an <code>n</code>-byte message; an empty message is still one block.
|-
|<code>SIGCHECK = 500000</code>
|An elliptic-curve operation: Schnorr verification, apart from its challenge hash, and fixed transaction-message preparation with a nonempty signature, zero with an empty signature; or a public key tweak.
|}

===Composing Opcode Costs===

Every executed opcode pays:
* <code>BASE</code>;
* <code>READ(length(X))</code> for each operand <code>X</code> it takes as a number, including the operands of the bitwise and shift opcodes, twice for an operand it compares twice;
* the primitives of its computation;
* <code>WRITE</code> for each value it produces.

An opcode produces a value when it copies or builds bytes into new storage, or when its result can be longer than the operand it is computed in.  An opcode that computes its result in an operand's storage, and whose result can never be longer than that operand whatever the operands' values, produces no value: its other charges cover the work.  A produced value pays <code>WRITE</code> once, and this covers its release.  As with every cost primitive, <code>WRITE</code> is priced from worst cases, which is why it is comparatively high: its per-byte rate is that of writing to newly allocated memory that the operating system must first provide.  An operand left on the stack unchanged is not produced, and OP_EQUALVERIFY, OP_NUMEQUALVERIFY and OP_CHECKSIGVERIFY produce no value.  A produced numeric result pays <code>WRITE</code> of its length, unless its opcode's formula specifies otherwise, as OP_MUL's does.

A literal push, including an empty push and OP_1 through OP_16, costs <code>BASE + WRITE(n)</code> for the <code>n</code> bytes it pushes.

==Opcode Costs==

The following tables give the costs of existing opcodes, with an analysis of how they apply.  [[bip-0441.mediawiki|BIP441]] adopts these charges as consensus rules in Tapleaf 0xC2; the opcodes it re-enables and the signature and timelock opcodes are costed there, and opcodes that another BIP defines are costed in that BIP.

The top of stack is labeled A, with successive values B, C, etc.  <code>length(X)</code> is the byte length of stack element <code>X</code>.  <code>value(X)</code> is <code>X</code> interpreted as a number, as [[bip-0441.mediawiki|BIP441]] specifies.  Operand lengths and values are those immediately before the opcode executes, unless a formula names its result.

===Control And Simple Examination Opcodes===

{|
! Opcode
! Varops Budget Cost
|-
|OP_VERIFY
|BASE + READ(length(A))
|-
|OP_NOT
|BASE + READ(length(A)) + WRITE(length(result))
|-
|OP_0NOTEQUAL
|BASE + READ(length(A)) + WRITE(length(result))
|-
|OP_EQUAL
|BASE + WRITE(length(result)) + (if length(A) = length(B): READ(length(A)); otherwise 0)
|-
|OP_EQUALVERIFY
|BASE + (if length(A) = length(B): READ(length(A)); otherwise 0)
|-
|OP_NOP, OP_NOP1, OP_NOP4 to OP_NOP10
|BASE
|-
|OP_IF, OP_NOTIF, OP_ELSE, OP_ENDIF
|BASE, also in inactive branches
|-
|OP_CODESEPARATOR
|BASE
|}

====Rationale====

OP_IF and OP_NOTIF take only minimal conditions, so they pay BASE alone.

OP_EQUAL and OP_EQUALVERIFY don't have to examine any data (and the Bitcoin Core implementation does not) if the lengths are different.

