Numerical comparison in little-endian numbers involves comparing the padded word spans, then if one is longer, checking that the remainder is all zero.

===Hash Operators===

{|
! Opcode
! Varops Budget Cost
|-
|OP_SHA256
|BASE + HASH(length(A)) + WRITE(32)
|-
|OP_HASH160
|BASE + HASH(length(A)) + HASH(32) + WRITE(20)
|-
|OP_HASH256
|BASE + HASH(length(A)) + HASH(32) + WRITE(32)
|-
|OP_RIPEMD160
|BASE + HASH(length(A)) + WRITE(20)
|-
|OP_SHA1
|BASE + HASH(length(A)) + WRITE(20)
|}

====Rationale====

SHA256 is hardware-accelerated on many current machines, but its price must also cover machines in use without SHA extensions, where its cost per byte is three to four times higher.  HASH covers SHA256 on those machines, and RIPEMD160 and SHA1 over the messages of up to 520 bytes that they accept ([https://github.com/jmoik/varopsData/blob/master/METHODOLOGY.md#combining-machines METHODOLOGY.md]).  Once-off steps such as the final SHA round are covered by the fixed term, and the second pass of HASH160 and HASH256 processes a 32-byte intermediate digest, not the original operand again.

BIP441's 520-byte limit for direct OP_SHA1 and OP_RIPEMD160 inputs is unchanged.

==Security Considerations==

Because the budget is shared, whether an input that charges it passes depends on the weight of the whole transaction and on the other inputs' charges, unlike the per-input budget of [[bip-0342.mediawiki|BIP342]].  An input near the limit can fail when a co-signer substitutes a smaller valid witness after the others have signed, when a co-signer's script costs more than the others assumed, or when a third party strips trailing zero bytes from a numeric witness element.  In the first and last cases the original transaction stays valid and can be rebroadcast; in the second it must be rebuilt.  No party gains funds, and a single party controlling every input avoids the first two cases.  Protocols whose scripts approach the budget should fund it from the weight of the inputs and outputs they control and use minimal numeric encodings.

